<!-- #endregion sigmoid -->
<!-- #region odds -->

| Group | Delinquent / total | p | odds = p/(1 − p) |
|---|---|--:|---|
| Normal testosterone | 402 / 4016 | 0.1001 | 0.1001 / 0.8999 = **0.111** |
| High testosterone | 101 / 446 | 0.2265 | 101/345 = **0.293** |

**Odds ratio** = 0.2928 / 0.1112 = **2.63**: the odds of delinquency are about 2.6 times higher in the high group. (The slide divides the rounded odds, 0.293/0.111, and gets 2.64. Either is fine; show the working.)

**Cancer study:** log odds = −2.6837 + 0.0812 × SurvRate.

| SurvRate | log odds (z) | odds = e^z | p = odds/(1 + odds) |
|:-:|---|---|---|
| 40 | −2.6837 + 0.0812 × 40 = 0.5643 | e^0.5643 = 1.758 | 0.6374 |
| 41 | −2.6837 + 0.0812 × 41 = 0.6455 | e^0.6455 = 1.907 | 0.656 |

Odds ratio for one extra unit: 1.907/1.758 = 1.0846 = e^0.0812: the odds rise by 8.5% per unit, while the probability only moves from 0.6374 to 0.656.

**Programming-task study:** b₀ = −3.0597, b₁ = 0.1615 (x = months of experience). p(14 months) = 1/(1 + e^−(−3.0597 + 0.1615 × 14)) = **0.31**. Odds ratio per month = e^0.1615 = **1.175** (+17.5% per month); over 15 months (10 vs 25) it is e^(15 × 0.1615) = **11.3**, an eleven-fold rise in the odds.

<!-- #endregion odds -->
<!-- #region logistic-gd -->

| x | y | z = wx + b | p = σ(z) | p − y | (p − y)x |
|:-:|:-:|--:|--:|--:|--:|
| 1 | 0 | 0 | 0.5 | 0.5 | 0.5 |
| 2 | 1 | 0 | 0.5 | −0.5 | −1 |
| 4 | 1 | 0 | 0.5 | −0.5 | −2 |
|  |  |  | **Σ** | **−0.5** | **−2.5** |

$$ \begin{aligned} \frac{\partial J}{\partial w} &= \tfrac{1}{m}\textstyle\sum (p - y)x = \tfrac{1}{3}(-2.5) = -0.8333, & w &\leftarrow 0 - 0.1(-0.8333) = \mathbf{0.0833} \\ \frac{\partial J}{\partial b} &= \tfrac{1}{m}\textstyle\sum (p - y) = \tfrac{1}{3}(-0.5) = -0.1667, & b &\leftarrow 0 - 0.1(-0.1667) = \mathbf{0.0167} \end{aligned} $$

Log loss falls from 0.6931 (that is ln 2: every p = 0.5) to 0.6278. The update has exactly the shape of linear regression’s; only ŷ = σ(z) is different.

<!-- #endregion logistic-gd -->
